WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners episode 101 for July 13th,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.740
2026. Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.440
Let's get into it. You know that moment when your navigation app re-routes you onto a busy road,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.940
even though you just wanted a quieter path. Today, the team at Waze, the navigation app owned by Google,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.880
started rolling out new voice features powered by Gemini, their AI assistant.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.760
Drivers can now say things like make this trip less chatty or avoid highways today,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.100
and the app understands the request in context without any tapping or menu hunting.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.860
Think of it like a passenger who knows your usual shortcuts and quietly adjust the map the

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:51.760
second you speak. Another way to picture it is a smart radio that listens to your casual request

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.200
and changes the station without forcing you to look away from the road. This matters because

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.740
navigation tools sit on millions of phones and get used during everyday routines like heading to

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:08.640
school, sports practice, or running errands. A voice that actually listens could cut down on

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.320
frustrating re-routes and let people decide how much the directions interrupt their music or podcasts.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.160
It also shows AI moving out of separate chat windows and into apps people already open every day.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.500
You can try the new features right now by opening the Waze app, starting any route,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.960
and speaking a request like make this trip less chatty. The app should respond directly with updated

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:39.780
directions and a quieter voice style. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show. The deep dive where

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:46.000
we look under the hood at how one piece of technology actually works. Today's deep dive comes from

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.940
the world of wearables like smart watches and phones that quietly track steps, heart rate, and sleep.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:58.560
The raw numbers sit there as streams of data and the question is how an AI turns those numbers into

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.340
useful health insights without a person labeling every single reading. Picture a huge library where

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:11.500
every book is a long string of numbers collected from millions of devices over years. A foundation

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:17.000
model for sensor data is like a librarian who has read every book and can spot patterns across

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.840
the entire collection at once. When a new reading arrives, the model compares it against everything

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:28.780
it has seen before. Much the way you might notice your own heart rate rises after certain meals or

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:34.480
that you sleep better on weekends. The difference is the model spots those patterns across millions

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.880
of people at the same time instead of just one person's experience. That comparison lets the system

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.980
guess what the new reading might mean for health or activity levels, such as spotting early signs

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:53.160
someone is getting sick or changing routines. The payoff is future apps that could offer tips based

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.260
on your own patterns rather than one size fits all advice. And the best part is you do not need to

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:05.600
understand the math to benefit from it. Next up, cool stuff and try this. Where we look at tools and

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.180
experiments, you can check out yourself. First, Instagram recently added a feature that let people

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:17.780
turn public photos into AI-generated images. Then quickly removed it after users pushed back.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.800
Meta added the tool to let users generate AI images from public photos on the platform.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.320
The feature was pulled after complaints about the way it used other people's content without

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:34.920
stronger consent choices. The company stated the goal was creative control for users.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.000
Listeners made clear they did not want their photos feeding the system in that manner.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.200
Your content is no longer being pulled into that particular AI tool if you post on Instagram.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.400
You can check your own settings by opening the app, tapping your profile, opening the menu and

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.060
looking under settings and privacy for any AI or content options. Second, a solar company called

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:05.740
Sunrun is testing small AI computers placed inside customers homes, paying homeowners for the space

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:12.400
and electricity used. Sunrun, known for home solar panels, is testing a program that places compact

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:19.040
AI computing units in customers houses. The company would pay homeowners for the electricity and space

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:25.720
those units use. The idea is to spread computing power across many locations instead of building

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:32.380
giant data centers. The same homes already running on solar could one day help power AI tools for

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:38.760
homework or creative projects. It is still early but the pilot focuses on distributed setups rather

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:44.940
than centralized facilities. If the pilot expands you might see offers through solar installers in

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:51.180
your area. Though right now it is mainly a wait and see development. Quick bits time where we hit

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:57.940
a couple of shorter stories. A study found roughly 41% of longer posts on LinkedIn look fully written

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:04.700
by AI higher than on other platforms. The study examined posts across five platforms and identified

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:12.360
LinkedIn as having the highest share of long form AI generated content. About 41% of longer posts on

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:18.420
the site appear fully generated by AI. This rate sits far above the levels found on the other

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.460
platforms included in the research. The findings serve as a reminder that some of what you scroll through

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:30.740
on social media may come from a model rather than a person typing the words. Finally, even though

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:36.420
Apple never released a self-driving car, the chip work from that project helped create the fast

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:43.600
on-device AI now running in iPhones and Macs. Apple worked on powerful chips intended for a self-driving

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.240
vehicle that was never released. The lessons from trying to run complex AI tasks without constant

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:56.160
